Given this file, the compiled JavaScript contains a variable named `API_URL`.

```ts
declare const API_URL: string;
export const url = API_URL + "/v1";
```

This global declaration file worked for months. After someone added the first line, `window.appVersion` now fails with TS2339 everywhere. Why, and what is the fix?

```ts
// globals.d.ts
import type { Foo } from "./foo";

interface Window {
  appVersion: string;
  foo: Foo;
}
```

A new file contains only this, and tsc reports TS2669: "Augmentations for the global scope can only be directly nested in external modules or ambient module declarations." What is the smallest correct fix?

```ts
// env.d.ts
declare global {
  interface Window { featureFlags: Record<string, boolean> }
}
```

A project types CSS modules with the declaration below. What happens when a component writes `className={styles.buton}` (a typo for `button`)?

```ts
declare module "*.module.css" {
  const classes: { readonly [key: string]: string };
  export default classes;
}
```

These three interface declarations merge. Put the resulting overloads of `clone` in the order the compiler checks them, first to last.

```ts
interface Cloner { clone(a: Animal): Animal; }
interface Cloner { clone(a: Sheep): Sheep; }
interface Cloner {
  clone(a: Dog): Dog;
  clone(a: Cat): Cat;
}
```

A consumer with `"moduleResolution": "bundler"` gets TS7016 for `import { x } from "pkg"`, even though `dist/index.d.ts` exists. Why?

```json
{
  "name": "pkg",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": { "import": "./dist/index.mjs", "require": "./dist/index.cjs" }
  }
}
```

Given this declaration in a module, which lines in `main.ts` type-check?

```ts
// globals.d.ts
declare global {
  var __APP_VERSION__: string;
  let __BUILD__: number;
}
export {};

// main.ts
const a = globalThis.__APP_VERSION__; // line 1
const b = globalThis.__BUILD__;       // line 2
const c = __BUILD__;                  // line 3
```

With `target: "es2022"` (so `useDefineForClassFields` is on), what does `new A().name` hold, and why does `declare` matter here?

```ts
class Base { name: string = "base"; }

class A extends Base {
  declare name: "a" | "base";
}
```

With `verbatimModuleSyntax` and ES module output, what is emitted for these two files? `./types` only exports an interface.

```ts
// a.ts
import type { User } from "./types";
export const a: User = { id: 1 };

// b.ts
import { type User } from "./types";
export const b: User = { id: 2 };
```

The untyped package `legacy` exports `slugify`. You add `legacy.d.ts` containing only the block below. Which two statements are true? (Choose two.)

```ts
declare module "legacy" {
  export function slugify(input: string, opts?: { lower?: boolean }): string;
}
```

The package `core` ships typings exporting `run`. To add one missing export, someone writes the file below with no imports or exports. Now `import { run, other } from "core"` fails with TS2305: Module '"core"' has no exported member 'run'. Why?

```ts
// shims.d.ts
declare module "core" {
  export function other(): void;
}
```

A library ships two versions of its typings. With tsc 6, which file does `import { which } from "lib"` read?

```json
{
  "name": "lib",
  "types": "index.d.ts",
  "typesVersions": {
    ">=5.0": { "*": ["ts5/*"] }
  }
}
```

What are the types of `r1` and `r2`?

```ts
interface A { f(x: string): "first"; }
interface A { f(x: string): "second"; }
interface A { g(x: string): "general"; }
interface A { g(x: "lit"): "literal"; }

declare const a: A;
const r1 = a.f("s");
const r2 = a.g("lit");
```
